1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,110
A hackathon winner opens one link, touches their fingerprint, and when the organizer approves their paperwork, fifteen thousand dollars lands in that exact account. In the same transaction. That's Claimdesk.

2
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:30,856
Today the biggest hackathon platform tells winners prize fulfillment can take up to sixty days. Bounty boards send a payment form and a KYC check. This hackathon's own rules say winners may be required to set up a wallet address.

3
00:00:31,455 --> 00:00:39,765
Every program that pays strangers runs this loop by hand, and the person whose paperwork was checked is never tied to the address that gets paid.

4
00:00:40,366 --> 00:00:55,936
I'm on the receiving side: a solo builder and engineering student in South Korea who sells software abroad. I've entered about a dozen global hackathons since August, each with different payout rules, and my own route home has a hundred-dollar minimum and no payout in won.

5
00:00:56,538 --> 00:01:08,518
The organizer locks every award on Tempo in one transaction. The winner opens their link once; the passkey becomes their account, and registering it is free — the organizer pays the fee.

6
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:24,120
The organizer checks the paperwork for that address, and clearing must name it. If a forwarded link registered first, the clearance is refused on-chain, and the organizer reissues the link. Tax withheld at source goes out in the same transaction.

7
00:01:24,722 --> 00:01:33,502
The winner gets a receipt in their own currency, and can move the money to a wallet or an exchange that accepts Tempo deposits, paying the tiny fee in the same dollars.

8
00:01:34,102 --> 00:01:46,642
Every piece is a Tempo protocol feature: transfer memos, native fee sponsorship, passkey accounts and batched calls. Every refusal in the demo is a real transaction on Tempo testnet.

9
00:01:47,245 --> 00:01:59,345
Claim links are instant but ungated. Payout platforms are gated but make the winner onboard. Claimdesk pays a stranger with no wallet, only after paperwork, to the account the paperwork was checked for.

10
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:19,697
We start where stablecoin programs already pay strangers: hackathons, bounties and ecosystem grants. One grant program the size of the Ethereum Foundation's paid out thirty-two point six million dollars in a single quarter. At a quarter percent plus a dollar per payout, three programs like that is a million a year.

11
00:02:20,297 --> 00:02:36,907
No users yet. In the next thirty days: one real program as a pilot, a KYC and tax-form provider wired to the clear button, and mainnet. If you run a prize, bounty or grant program, we'd like to pay your next winners. Claimdesk: pay every winner by link.
